>> I want to run 4.3BSD on my mVAXII, but have no tapedrive on it.  My only
>> tape is on the 8600 running 5.0, VMS that is.  The 4.3BSD tape contains 9
>> files.

That's going to be tough.  Reading the files is easy; there's a public
"VMS untar" (look in anon ftp area of gatekeeper.dec.com, in /pub/VMS)
that will read a tar tape for you and unpack the files onto a VMS disk,
for what little use that will be to you.

The problem here is how to get these files onto a MicroVAX disk in UNIX
format.  To do that, you're going to need to run one of the programs
from the first part of the tape -- it's a standalone copy utility.
Unfortunately, this standalone copy utility wants to copy from tape to
disk, not from a remote VMS system to a local disk.  It just isn't that
smart.

There's a chance, if you can get the raw tarfiles onto your VMS system
(note, actually, that they aren't all tarfiles, some are standalone
format/copy utilities and at least one is a "dump" image of the root),
you might somehow get them over to a TK50 drive on some other system.
Trouble is, early 4.3bsd doesn't boot off a TK50.

I think you're going to have to boot this tape up onto a spare disk drive
on your 8600, and using the 8600-running-4.3bsd, build up the TK50 boot
tape you'll need using bit tweezers.  You'd have an easier time if you
called Mt Xinu or Digital and got a commerical BSD for your Microvax,
but of course those avenues cost money.
