Some details about your questions regarding ETHER-net thinwire 
to twisted pair connection.

The solution, proposed by "the net", with a DESPR (single port 
repeater) would work fine. The DESPR has got one "15-pin D-connector"
to interface your transciver (Synoptics, in this case) and one 
thinwire segment connector. This is a fully normal thinwire ETHER-
net connetion (max 185 m length, max 30 connections ...), that most
likely would suite Your office for a while. An alternative (if You
plan to grow -- :-) -- is the DEMPR, an identical repeater, but 
supporting 8 separate thinwire-segments.

Note the difference between transciever and repeater. The fact that
two transcivers both have a 15-pin "D" type connector does't mean
You can directly connect them! For instance: Transcievers require power 
supply from the computer/bridge/repeater/... The proposed "cheap" 
soloution with 2 transcivers (one is Your Synoptics, one DESTA) can't
possibly work (who will supply power? Both transceievers want to take
power from the oter!), less the Synoptics is not a transciever, but a 
repeater, but in that case You would have no problem, and most likely
would not have asked the question... 

Also note the restrictons on all ETHER-net systems. You must not
connect to many repeaters in series on the network. Check if your
twisted-pair network supports more repeaters, or if it already is
to late. If it does not, You would have to put an ETHER-net bridge
somewhere in Your net (?? $5000 ??). Quite a cost! If it is in that
way, I can only give You my best congratulations, painting Yourself
into a corner by purchasing the "cheap" twisted pair cable that not
yet is widely spread, and just newly standardized, instead of purchasing
an "of the shelf" thinwire system. In this corner of the world (Sweden)
the difference in price is almost nothing, especialy becauce the twisted 
pair requires one "output port" to each connected pice of equipment, 
compared with the max. 30 connections for each thinwire.

Also note that thinwire connected VT-1000 have internal transciever, giving
you a $300(tcvr) + $80(AUI-cable) (US prices??) saving for each X-terminal, 
compared with the twisted-pair network strategy. $380 /terminal could pay
quite some cabling work, I guess.

Myabe, the DEC twisted pair equipment newly mentioned would be better,
or maybe an 3-party soloution wold be fine for You. For instance
